.TH CYAML_CLONE 3 "2026-09-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_clone \- produce a deep independent copy of a DOM subtree
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cyaml.h>

cyaml cyaml_clone(cyaml node);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Recursively copy node and all of its descendants.  The clone uses the
same allocator as the source tree, and each node's own tag (see
cyaml_node_tag(3)), if any, is carried over unchanged.  Mutating the
clone does not affect the original and vice versa.

node need not have come from cyaml_parse*(); a tree built directly via
cyaml_create_list_mp(3)/cyaml_dictionary_set(3) is subject to the same
500-level nesting limit cyaml_parse*(3) enforces at parse time (also
the same limit cyaml_serialize(3)/cyaml_serialize_flow(3) enforce
independently). If such a tree is nested past that limit, this
function returns NULL rather than recursing without bound.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
node  Root of the subtree to copy.  NULL is accepted and returns NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Root of the new tree (caller owns it), or NULL on OOM or if node is
nested more than 500 levels deep (the two failure causes are
indistinguishable, since this function has no separate error-string
channel).
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_destroy (3),
.BR cyaml_node_tag (3)
